.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-UPDATE-APPLICATION 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-update-application \- change an application's definition
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap update-application
.BR \-\-application\-id | \-n
.I NAME
[\fB\-\-runtime\fR|\fB\-r\fR \fIRUNTIME\fR]
[\fB\-\-artifact\fR|\fB\-a\fR \fIKEY\fR]
[\fB\-\-command\fR|\fB\-c\fR \fICMD\fR]
[\fB\-\-arguments\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-environment\fR|\fB\-e\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-buckets\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-queues\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-manifest\fR \fIDIR\fR]
[\fB\-\-namespace\fR \fINAME\fR]
[\fB\-\-node\-labels\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-min\-instances\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-max\-instances\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-type\fR \fIPROCESS|JOB\fR]
[\fB\-\-schedule\fR \fICRON\fR]
[\fB\-\-memory\-limit\fR \fISIZE\fR]
[\fB\-\-cpu\-limit\fR \fICORES\fR]
[\fB\-\-ready\-timeout\fR \fIMS\fR]
[\fB\-\-user\fR|\fB\-u\fR \fIUSERID\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Changes an existing application's definition. Only the options actually
given are altered, so one setting can be changed without resending the
whole definition;
.B \-\-arguments
and
.B \-\-environment
replace the current values rather than adding to them.
.PP
A running application is restarted to pick the change up, within a few
seconds: the artifact, the command, the environment and the credentials
a process was handed are all decided when it starts, so there is nothing
a running process could apply. Redeploying a new build of the same
artifact key is therefore an upload followed by this command.
.PP
Unlike
.BR euclid-cli-eap-redeploy-application (1),
nothing here is checked against the build already deployed: this is how a
version is deliberately rebuilt in place, or rolled back to bytes already
seen. Changing
.B \-\-artifact
re-reads the artifact's MD5 onto the definition, so what it records is
always the artifact it points at. The recorded version is left as it
was; to record a new one, use
.BR euclid-cli-eap-redeploy-application (1).
.PP
.B \-\-user
points the application at a different EAM identity. It has to exist and
hold an access key, checked here rather than discovered later: an
application pointed at a principal that cannot authenticate starts
normally and is refused everything it calls, and what it reports is the
first call it made \- a secret it could not read, a queue it could not
reach \- rather than who it was calling as.
.PP
That is the repair for a definition naming a principal that has been
deleted or renamed, which before this could only be fixed by deleting the
application and creating it again.
.PP
If the application was running as a technical principal EAP issued it
(\fIapp\-<runtime name>\fR), that principal is deleted with the change,
its grants first \- it was made for this application, nothing else can
use it, and a grant left pointing at a deleted principal comes back to
life the day somebody creates a user of that name again. A user you named
yourself is yours and is left alone.
.PP
The application's bucket cannot be changed here; the artifact key must
name an object in the bucket the application already has.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-application\-id " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the application to change. Required.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-runtime " " \fIRUNTIME\fR
What the artifact is started with: \fIJAVA\fR, \fIJAVA21\fR,
\fIJAVA25\fR, \fIPYTHON\fR, \fINODEJS\fR, \fIBINARY\fR or \fIRUST\fR. Changing it
restarts the application's instances under the new one.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-artifact " " \fIKEY\fR
Object key of the artifact within the application's bucket. Must exist.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIUSERID\fR
EAM user the application runs as. Must exist and have an access key.
Replaces the technical principal EAP issued the application, if it had
one \- see above.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-command " " \fICMD\fR
Command to run instead of the runtime's default.
.TP
.B \-\-arguments " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated arguments passed after the artifact path; replaces the
current list.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-environment " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated \fIKEY=value\fR pairs; replaces the current set.
.TP
.B \-\-buckets " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated bucket names the application may use; replaces the
current list. The application's principal is re-granted, so the change
takes effect at once - running instances included, since this is
enforced where a request is served rather than where a process starts.
.TP
.B \-\-queues " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated queue names the application may use; replaces the
current list.
.TP
.B \-\-manifest " " \fIDIR\fR
An application's
.I euclid/
directory. The objects it declares become the resources the application
is granted, instead of every resource in its namespace. See
.BR euclid-cli-eap-apply (1).
.TP
.B \-\-namespace " " \fINAME\fR
Namespace the application's own requests run in, which lets it name a
queue or topic instead of spelling out a full ERN. An empty string moves
it back to the account root.
.TP
.B \-\-node\-labels " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated \fIkey=value\fR labels a worker node must carry to be
given the application, for example \fBos=windows\fR; replaces the current
set. An empty string clears it, so the application may be placed on any
node again. See
.BR euclid-cli-eap-create-application (1).
.TP
.B \-\-min\-instances " " \fIN\fR
Smallest number of instances the autoscaler keeps running.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-instances " " \fIN\fR
Largest number of instances the autoscaler may scale out to.
.TP
.B \-\-type " " \fIPROCESS|JOB\fR
.I PROCESS
for something that stays up, or
.I JOB
for something that runs once and finishes. A JOB is not restarted when it
exits with status 0, and is not autoscaled.
.TP
.B \-\-schedule " " \fICRON\fR
Cron expression a JOB runs on, in UTC, for example
.B \(dq0 2 * * *\(dq
or
.BR @daily .
Only valid with
.BR "\-\-type JOB" ;
an empty value unschedules it.
.TP
.B \-\-memory\-limit " " \fISIZE\fR
The most memory one instance may use, as for
.BR euclid-cli-eap-create-application (1).
Through this command the change restarts the application, like any other
change to its definition, which is how running instances \- whose JVM sized
its heap from the old limit \- get the new one.
.BR euclid-cli-eap-set-application-limits (1)
changes it without a restart.
.TP
.B \-\-cpu\-limit " " \fICORES\fR
The most CPU one instance may use, in cores; 0 for none. As for
.BR \-\-memory\-limit ,
this restarts the application; set-application-limits does not.
.TP
.B \-\-ready\-timeout " " \fIMS\fR
Recorded on the definition, and no longer used to decide anything - see
.BR euclid-cli-eap-create-application (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The definition was changed. It is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the caller is not an authenticated
administrator, no application of that name exists, the runtime is not
one of the four, the artifact does not exist in the application's
bucket, or a bucket or queue named by \fB\-\-buckets\fR/\fB\-\-queues\fR
does not exist.
.SH EXAMPLES
Deploy a new build:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm upload-file --bucket apps --key inbox.jar --file target/inbox.jar
euclid-cli eap update-application --application-id inbox --artifact inbox.jar
.fi
.RE
.PP
Let it scale further:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap update-application --application-id inbox --max-instances 8
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run it on Windows workers only, and later anywhere again:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap update-application --application-id inbox --node-labels os=windows
euclid-cli eap update-application --application-id inbox --node-labels ""
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-redeploy-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-create-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-get-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-upload-file (1)
